Context (pp. 36-38): H is the 3-strand braid group B_3 = <e0,e1 | e0 e1 e0 = e1 e0 e1>. It is the Teichmüller (mapping class) group of the torus with one boundary component, a central extension of SL(2,Z) = pi_1 of the moduli stack M_{1,1}. The notes write e for epsilon and r for rho. The distinguished elements are:
- the Dehn twists e0, e1;
- s = e0 e1 e0 (sigma), whose square generates the centre;
- r0 = (e1 e0)^{-1} and r1 = (e0 e1)^{-1}, of order 3 modulo the centre;
- r'0 = (e1 e0)^2 = r0^{-2} and r'1 = (e0 e1)^2 = r1^{-2}.
Grothendieck runs through every 'principal' pair of these elements that generates H. For each pair he expresses the other elements in it and writes defining relations. Page 36 did the pairs (e0,e1), (s,e0), (s,r0), (s,r'0) and (e0,r0). This page does three more.

Case (e0, r'0). Since r0^3 is central, conjugation by r'0 = r0^{-2} equals conjugation by r0. So e1 = r'0 e0 r'0^{-1}. The other elements are:
- s = r'0 e0^{-1};
- r0 = e0 r'0^{-1} e0;
- r1 = e0^2 r'0^{-1};
- r'1 = e0 r'0 e0^{-1}.
All of these check in B_3. The boxed relations are r'0 = (r'0(e0) e0)^2, i.e. r'0 = (r'0 e0 r'0^{-1} e0)^2, together with [r'0^3, e0] = 1. The NB then rewrites the relations through the single commutator [A, e0] = 1 with A = r'0(e0) e0 r'0. Using r'0 = (r'0(e0) e0)^2, A equals (r'0(e0) e0)^3 = (e1 e0)^3, which is central.

Case (e0, r1), labelled h'0. Grothendieck notes it reduces to (h0) by conjugation and that one should rather take (e1, r0). Here:
- e1 = r1^{-1} e0 r1;
- s = r1^{-1} e0;
- r0 = e0^{-1} r1 e0;
- r'0 = e0^{-1} r1^{-2} e0;
- r'1 = r1^{-2}.
The boxed relation is r1 e0 r1^{-1} e0 r1 = 1. Setting b = r1^{-1} = e0 e1, this is exactly x b x = b^2 with x = e0, which is equivalent to the braid relation. So <e0, r1 | r1 e0 r1^{-1} e0 r1> is a correct one-relator presentation of B_3.

Case (e0, r'1), again labelled j'0 in the image. Here:
- e1 = r'1^{-1} e0 r'1;
- s = e0^{-1} r'1;
- r1 = e0 r'1^{-1} e0;
- r'0 = e0^{-1} r'1 e0;
- r0 = r'1^{-1} e0^2.
The boxed relations are r'1 = (r'1 e0 r'1^{-1} e0)^2 and [r'1^3, e0] = 1. Indeed r'1(e0) e0 = e0 e1, so the first relation holds.

Each case ends with 'symmetric formulas' obtained by the involutions written out on p. 38: g -> g-check, and int(s), which swaps e0 and e1. The purpose is to build a catalogue of the 'principal systems of generators' of ST_{11}, i.e. the genus-1, one-point Teichmüller group. That catalogue feeds Grothendieck's description of the Teichmüller tower and its action of Gal(Q-bar/Q), the 'Teichmüllerie' of Esquisse d'un programme.
